1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
How to Plan Your Strength Training Week, written by David Robertson, online strength coach.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:10,640
Already following a strength program, but struggling to fit its sessions into your week.

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,640
Start by comparing the training you planned with the training you completed.

4
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,840
How do I fit my strength program into the week?

5
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,880
The biggest planning mistake I see in strength training is committing to a training program

6
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:28,000
without deciding when its sessions will happen. Saying you will train four times a week gives you

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,960
a number. Putting those four sessions on specific days gives you a schedule you can review.

8
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,480
At the end of the week, you can compare what you intended to do with what you actually completed

9
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:44,160
and see how well the plan fits your circumstances. Take Alex, a fictional executive who wants

10
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:49,680
to squat more weight for five reps. His program calls for four strength sessions a week,

11
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:56,480
including two lower body sessions. He puts them on Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday,

12
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:01,360
and commits to that schedule for the next three weeks. At four sessions a week,

13
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:06,320
he has 12 planned workouts. That gives him a clear starting point for checking how consistently

14
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:12,960
he follows the program. How do I build my weekly training matrix? A training matrix is a simple

15
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:19,120
table showing which workout belongs on which day. Start with the frequency specified in your program.

16
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:24,000
Then look at the days and time you have available. Include the time each session needs.

17
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:31,040
For a four day split like Alex's, I usually recommend Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday.

18
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:36,960
Here is how those sessions fit together. Each of the three weeks follows the same schedule. Monday

19
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:42,480
is the first upper body session. Tuesday is the first lower body session. Wednesday is a day off.

20
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:48,800
Thursday is the second upper body session, and Friday is the second lower body session. Saturday

21
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:55,440
and Sunday are days off. This is an illustrative structure, session names, order, frequency,

22
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:01,440
and spacing depend on the trainee and the program. Before the three weeks begin, check for meetings,

23
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:06,960
deadlines, and travel you already know about. Then look back at the last few weeks, which days

24
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:12,160
were hardest to keep free for training. If Mondays and Thursdays are likely to be busy,

25
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:17,440
Alex could choose Tuesday, Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday before the block begins,

26
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:22,240
keeping the workouts in the same order. The plan still includes four strength sessions

27
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:27,680
each week, now on days that better fit his schedule. Your training week needs room for more than

28
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:34,560
workouts. Work, preparing food, time with other people, time alone, and winding down before

29
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:40,320
sleep all affect what fits. Consider the fatigue from training alongside the demands of the rest of

30
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:45,760
your life. When the same session repeatedly gets missed, review whether it's timing and demands

31
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:51,200
fit your week. Aim for a sustainable rhythm that allows for recovery and unexpected changes.

32
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:57,360
What should I record after each session? Keep your plan matrix and record what happened alongside

33
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:03,360
it. For each session, note whether you completed it, the day you trained, and any reason it was

34
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:10,800
missed, moved, or shortened. Keep recording your exercises, weights, sets, and reps in your workout

35
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:16,560
log. If you move Tuesday's workout to Wednesday, record both the plan day and the actual day.

36
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,320
You still completed the workout, but the original Tuesday slot did not fit that week as well as

37
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:28,560
you expected. That distinction helps you choose a better schedule at your review. Likewise,

38
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:34,160
mark a shortened session as shortened and note what you left out. A weekly count is useful,

39
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:38,400
but it will not tell you whether you repeatedly missed the part of the program that matters most

40
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:45,280
to your goal. What if I miss one of my planned days? On Tuesday, Alex's leadership meeting moves

41
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:50,960
into the time he set aside for his lower body session. By the time he gets home, he has a short window

42
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:56,960
before dinner. He does a 20-minute conditioning workout at home and keeps his next lower body session

43
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:02,240
on Friday. He records Tuesday's strength session as missed and logs the conditioning separately.

44
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,560
He stayed active, but he did not complete the strength workout in his matrix.

45
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:11,680
Keeping that record accurate is what makes the review useful. For this example,

46
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,640
I'd keep the rest of his week in place. Trying to fit the missed leg workout into the weekend would

47
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:21,840
put it closer to Friday's session than planned. Whether to move a workout depends on the program

48
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:27,120
and the time available to recover. One miss session is also worth distinguishing from a recurring

49
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:33,760
problem. If Tuesday keeps getting interrupted, write that down. At the next review, you can decide

50
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:39,200
whether Tuesday is a realistic training day and adjust the schedule accordingly. What can three

51
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:44,960
weeks tell me about my schedule? Check your record at the end of each week, then review the three weeks

52
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:52,000
together. For Alex, an illustrative record might look like this. In week one, Alex planned four

53
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,680
strength sessions and completed all four on the planned days. In week two, he planned four

54
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:03,680
and completed three, missing Tuesday because of a meeting. In week three, he planned four and

55
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:10,320
completed three, missing Tuesday because of a work deadline. Across the three weeks, he planned 12

56
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:17,600
strength sessions and completed 10. Alex completed 10 of 12 planned strength sessions, or about 83%.

57
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:23,200
More useful than the percentage alone is the pattern both missed sessions were Tuesday's lower

58
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:29,040
body workout. His overall attendance hides a repeated gap in the work tied to his squat goal.

59
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,560
Ask yourself. How many of my planned sessions did I complete each week? Did I train on the days I

60
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:42,400
chose? Or did I repeatedly move sessions? Did the same workout or part of a workout keep getting

61
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:48,000
missed? Look on in the way and what could I change for the next three weeks? Three weeks gives you

62
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,880
a practical starting point for reviewing how your schedule fits your life. It will not

63
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:59,040
capture every kind of disruption, so keep reviewing as your circumstances change. Use the record

64
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:04,160
alongside your workout log to understand both what you completed and how your training is progressing.

65
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:10,160
What should I change for the next three weeks? If you completed the sessions on your chosen days

66
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:15,840
and the schedule still fits, you have a reason to keep those days. You do not need to rearrange

67
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:22,000
the week simply because a review date has arrived. If a particular day repeatedly proved difficult,

68
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,400
look for a more reliable slot. Alex might move to Monday, Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday if

69
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,760
those days fit his upcoming commitments better. Write the revised schedule down before the next

70
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:39,920
three weeks begin so you can review how the revised schedule works. If you plan four sessions each

71
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:46,480
week but consistently completed two, look at why. Was it the days, the length of the sessions,

72
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:52,560
or the total time the program required? Review the program against the time and recovery you have

73
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:58,080
available. Simply leaving out half of a four day split may repeatedly leave out the same training.

74
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:03,760
Keep the original plan in your record. Changing next week's schedule does not change what you

75
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:09,840
planned or completed last week. How does this lead into periodization? Once you know which sessions you

76
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:15,040
can reliably attend, you have a clearer starting point for planning how the training itself changes

77
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:21,600
over time. Periodization means planning changes in training over time, including the amount of work

78
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:27,440
and how heavy it is to support a goal. Your weekly matrix sets out when the sessions happen,

79
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:33,600
the program sets out the work within them. At a review, use attendance, work out results,

80
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:39,360
and recovery together to guide the next phase. A repeated scheduling problem and a need to change

81
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:44,320
the training load call for different decisions. Keeping a record helps you see which question you need

82
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:49,920
to address. What should I put in my matrix today? Write down your training goal and the weekly

83
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:56,000
frequency your program specifies. Choose the days and times you will train, put each session in

84
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,400
the matrix, and set a review date three weeks from now. Then keep a record. At your review,

85
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:07,520
you will have a clearer answer to a practical question, how well did my plan training fit my week?

86
